It would be better to have a random sample of students, it would also be nice to have the backpacks weighed in the same spot each time so that surface differences wouldn’t affect the scale’s measurements.

## Summary

In many situations the mean will not be the primary statistic of interest. When exploring other statistics, like a median, we do not have a theory-based result for how the sampling distribution will behave. One method for exploring the sampling distribution of the statistic is bootstrapping. The idea behind bootstrapping is to sample from a population that is constructed from infinitely many copies of the observed sample. You explored this by randomly sampling, with replacement, from the observed sample. The key information from this bootstrap distribution is the standard deviation. This tells us how much sample-to-sample variation we can expect in the statistic from the random sampling process alone. Knowing the standard deviation gives you a way of measuring whether the observed statistic is "far" from the hypothesized value.


